Usage Note:
iproute2 and nftables are installed as separate programs; the daemon
invokes ip as a subprocess, and firewall-bridge runs nft as one, as its
own notice states. libnftables1 is the library nft is linked against.
These and nginx are installed from Debian's package repository when the
images are built, and the published images carry them. MANIFEST.json in
tools/compose lists every Debian package in the images with its source
files; ghcr.io/aras-workspace/phantom-sources holds those files under the
image version.

================================================================================
SOURCE CODE OF THE IMAGES
================================================================================

ghcr.io/aras-workspace/phantom-sources holds the Debian source files of the
published images, one tag per image version. tools/compose/MANIFEST.json
lists every package in the images and names the matching sources_image.

This image is an archive, not a program. It is built from scratch and holds
files only, so docker run has nothing to start and no shell to open. It is
not needed to run Phantom-WG, and the stack does not pull it.

To read it, create a container without starting it and copy the files out.
Replace <version> with the image version in tools/compose/registry.yml. The
word none is a placeholder: docker create asks for a command, and this one
never runs.

    docker create --name phantom-src \
      ghcr.io/aras-workspace/phantom-sources:<version> none
    docker cp phantom-src:/sources ./sources
    docker container rm phantom-src

./sources holds the .dsc, .orig.tar and .debian.tar files of each source
package, with SHA256SUMS next to them. To check the files, then unpack a
package on Debian (dpkg-source comes with dpkg-dev):

    cd sources
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
    dpkg-source -x <package>_<version>.dsc

